copt, BCC's peephole optimizer, for XENIX: K&R C, so that BCC compiles it,
and an input taken a piece at a time, so that it runs in a 16-bit
program's 64K of data.

Dev86's copt read the whole input, applied the first rules file to it, then
the next, and so on, and kept every line it read or made in a hash table
for good. Now it reads the rules files first, then optimizes the input one
piece at a time with each set of rules in turn, writes the piece out and
frees it. A piece ends before a global label (_name:), which no rule
matches, so no rule could have matched lines on both sides of it; a piece
of a very large function also ends after 1000 lines or 12000 bytes, as the
same build does on the host, so that both give the same code. The output
is the same as Dev86's copt gives for all of this project's sources and
all of BCC's (185 files, with both Dev86's rules and patch 11's).

Also: variables matched by a rule are kept in a fixed buffer rather than
in the hash table (a rule whose variables would not fit there does not
match); a line and its text are one allocation; optline() reports a match
rather than having optimize() look at a line it has freed, and a match at
the very start of a piece continues from there, where Dev86's copt
stopped optimizing the rest of the file; a rule whose first line cannot
match the line at hand is skipped before its variables are cleared, which
makes copt nearly twice as fast on an 8086.

